  "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266767/assets/da8f9302098e74dc26c360207d9062d96ac14d17299cf5a2993d70ce45fc69b5.png)\n\nThe law of sines states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡B}{b}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡A}{a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\,m∠A={114}^{∘},\\,b=5,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡B}{5} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{114}^{∘}}{9} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡B & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{114}^{∘}}{9} \\\\  & ≈5\\cdot \\frac{0.913\\,55}{9} \\\\  & ≈0.507\\,53,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & ≈\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.507\\,53)≈{30.5}^{∘},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ≈{180}^{∘}-m∠A-m∠B \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}-{114}^{∘}-{30.5}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{35.5}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnother solution to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡B≈0.507\\,53$ is given by\n\n$$\nm∠B≈{180}^{∘}-{30.5}^{∘}≈{149.5}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nBut we know that a triangle cannot have two obtuse angles. Therefore, there is no triangle that has two angles whose measures are ${114}^{∘}$ and ${149.5}^{∘}.$\n\nSo, there is only one possible triangle, with the following angle measures:\n\n$$\nm∠A={114}^{∘},\\,m∠B≈{30.5}^{∘},\\,m∠C≈{35.5}^{∘}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266767/assets/88208a1751e4af718bac2c58dac144034c6a92d9d5faaf2b9a6861b7f44f32f5.png)\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
